//? Title: Run TensorFlow Lite inference with reusable LiteRT buffers
//? Subtitle: Compile a converted model once and invoke its CPU signature repeatedly
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Load the converted affine TensorFlow Lite model, allocate the buffers
required by its compiled CPU signature, and compare repeated predictions
with the Python reference. A second example illustrates stable caller
buffers using Datamunge's eager affine operation.
)
\toc

> Run the converted model

The fixture was generated by `scripts/train_export_affine.py --tflite`
in the TensorFlow examples checkout. Its SavedModel and Python reference
outputs are the same files used in the neighboring SavedModel report.
LiteRT loads the converted FlatBuffer and compiles the named signature
for CPU execution. The runtime chooses concrete shapes of [1, 2] and
[1, 1] even though the original batch axis is dynamic.

Build Datamunge with `-DDATAMUNGE_ENABLE_LITERT=ON` and set
`CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` to the LiteRT C SDK prefix. The runtime determines
buffer capacities. Datamunge keeps the compiled model and both buffers
across invocations, and each returned tensor owns a copy of the result.
This example keeps the initial batch size of one.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <fstream>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  if (!n::lite_model_runtime_available()) {
    std::println("LiteRT requires a Datamunge build with DATAMUNGE_ENABLE_LITERT.");
    return 0;
  }
  const std::string fixture = "fixtures/python_affine/";
  const auto path = fixture + "model.tflite";
  const n::LiteModel model(path);
  const auto capacities = model.buffer_sizes();
  if (model.input_shape() != n::Shape{1, 2} || model.output_shape() != n::Shape{1, 1} ||
      capacities[0] < 2 * sizeof(float) || capacities[1] < sizeof(float))
    throw std::runtime_error("Unexpected compiled model contract");

  std::ifstream table(fixture + "reference.tsv");
  std::vector<std::array<float, 3>> rows;
  std::array<float, 3> row;
  while (table >> row[0] >> row[1] >> row[2]) rows.push_back(row);
  if (!table.eof() || rows.size() != 4) throw std::runtime_error("Missing Python reference");
  rows.push_back(rows.front());

  std::println("Model size {} bytes. Input and output capacities {} and {} bytes.\n",
               std::filesystem::file_size(path), capacities[0], capacities[1]);
  std::println("| Feature 0 | Feature 1 | Prediction | Python reference |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
  double max_error = 0.;
  for (const auto [a, b, expected] : rows) {
    const auto prediction = model.predict(n::tensor({a, b}, {1, 2}));
    const double actual = prediction.item();
    max_error = std::max(max_error, std::abs(actual - expected));
    if (prediction.requires_grad() || std::abs(actual - (.75 * a - 1.5 * b + .25)) > 1e-5)
      throw std::runtime_error("Unexpected affine prediction");
    std::println("| {} | {} | {:.7f} | {:.7f} |", a, b, actual, expected);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string invalid_path = "build/invalid_lite_fixture.tflite";
  std::ofstream invalid(invalid_path, std::ios::binary);
  constexpr std::array<char, 16> zeros{};
  invalid.write(zeros.data(), zeros.size());
  invalid.close();
  if (!invalid) throw std::runtime_error("Could not create malformed fixture");
  bool rejected = false;
  try {
    (void)n::LiteModel(invalid_path);
  } catch (const std::runtime_error&) {
    rejected = true;
  }
  if (!rejected || max_error > 1e-6) throw std::runtime_error("LiteRT parity check failed");
  std::println("\nMaximum Python error {:.2g}. Malformed FlatBuffer rejected.", max_error);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Model size 1044 bytes. Input and output capacities 8 and 4 bytes.

| Feature 0 | Feature 1 | Prediction | Python reference |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 0.2499999 | 0.2499999 |
| 1 | 0 | 0.9999993 | 0.9999993 |
| 0 | 1 | -1.2499989 | -1.2499989 |
| 2 | -3 | 6.2499952 | 6.2499952 |
| 0 | 0 | 0.2499999 | 0.2499999 |

Maximum Python error 0. Malformed FlatBuffer rejected.
// result_end

> The method

For one row with two Float32 inputs and one output, the public buffers contain 8 and 4 bytes.
The consumer keeps their tensor handles alive and replaces contents in place. The same affine
weights are reused for every call:

$$
\mathrm{output}_{1\times1}\leftarrow\mathrm{input}_{1\times2}W_{2\times1}+b_1.
$$
\alttext(The output buffer receives the two-feature affine computation without replacing its storage.)

A NoGradGuard prevents graph recording. affine_inference returns a temporary tensor, which is
copied into the persistent output buffer; matrix multiplication may allocate temporaries. Thus
stable public addresses do not establish allocation-free inference, compilation, quantization,
delegate acceleration or TFLite compatibility. Model initialization and input validation occur
before the repeated calls; each call costs O(input_width times output_width) in this fixture.

The concept diagram below is drawn by `dm::graphics::Diagram`. Its editable C++
source is the complete block below; regeneration uses the same datamunge toolchain
as the numerical examples. Exports consume the saved local SVG.

```{cpp, file="figures/tflite_inference_concept.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  diagram.node("n0", {0.0, 0}, "Persistent input\n2 Float32 = 8 bytes");
  diagram.node("n1", {5.2, 0}, "Native affine function\ntemporary result");
  diagram.node("n2", {10.4, 0}, "Persistent output\n1 Float32 = 4 bytes");
  diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
  diagram.save_svg("build/tflite_inference_concept.svg");
}
```
